// The two reaches a module two levels down may not write.
//
// Its parent, which is a cycle back along the edge that contains it. Containment
// runs one way: a parent reaches into its children by path, a child does not
// reach out, and it does not need to -- what the parent publishes is in the
// registry the child already reads.
//
// And its parent's SISTER, which is what SCOPED rather than accumulating means.
// The registry is rebuilt at each level from that level's own neighbourhood: my
// sisters, my children, and what my parent published. Descending into outer,
// beta drops out of reach and outer's children come in. A module here that needs
// beta says so to outer, and outer re-exports it.
//
// Scoped is adopted provisionally and is to be revisited:
// Ruminations\modules-design-brief.md, decision 10.

mod inner;

import outer;                 //~ ErrorModReach "may not import the module that contains it"
import "../../beta/beta";     //~ ErrorModReach "reached by its name"

fn reach(n i64) i64 {
  n;
}
